    "precision": "inferred",
    "sources": [
     "finney-1979-way-to-tahiti",
     "lewis-1976-natgeo",
     "pvs-newsletter-1975-08"
    ],
    "tellings": [
     {
      "source": "pvs-newsletter-1975-08",
      "says": "An overall length of 60 feet and a beam of 15 feet; each hull 3.5 feet wide and five feet deep; ten crosspieces, each 17 feet long; about five tons, carrying up to 7.5 tons more; two masts, each with a single Polynesian sprit sail."
     },
     {
      "source": "finney-1992-sea-to-space",
      "says": "Two hulls each 62 feet in length, eight crossbeams, decking, rails and two masts; the hulls of frames covered with layers of plywood strips."
     },
     {
      "source": "lewis-1976-natgeo",
      "says": "17 men aboard a 60-foot canoe."
     },
     {
      "source": "lewis-1977-topics",
      "says": "There were 17 men (including two photographers) aboard, plus the poi dog Hoku, the pig Maxwell and a cock and a hen."
     },
     {
      "source": "pvs-archive-1976",
      "says": "The crew from Hawaiʻi to Tahiti: Mau Piailug, navigator; Kawika Kapahulehua, captain; Clifford Ah Mow, Shorty Bertelmann, Ben Finney, Tommy Holmes, Sam Kalalau, Boogie Kalama, Buffalo Keaulana, John Kruse, Dukie Kuahulu, David Lewis, Dave Lyman, Billy Richards and Rodo Williams."
     },
     {
      "source": "mauinews-2021-hokulea",
      "says": "The National Geographic photographer Nicholas DeVore and the filmmaker Dale Bell were also aboard."
     }
    ],
    "note": "At anchor off the beach at the head of the bay, a line run to the shore, as Finney has her at the moment of casting off; the National Geographic account has her anchor raised as the sails are let out. Where in the bay she lay is not given: here a little off the beach at the stream mouth, the line within reach of the shore."
   },
