    "tellings": [
     {
      "source": "lewis-1976-natgeo",
      "says": "Far behind followed Meotai, a large ketch that would “shadow” us during the entire voyage; she was strictly forbidden to provide any navigational data or hint of our position."
     },
     {
      "source": "lewis-1977-topics",
      "says": "The 60 foot auxiliary ketch Meotai, the ‘chase boat’, followed the canoe."
     },
     {
      "source": "finney-1979-way-to-tahiti",
      "says": "The Meotai, the big steel-hulled motor sailer that is trailing us."
     },
     {
      "source": "samlow-moolelo-hokulea",
      "says": "Alex and Elsa Jakubenko built a 65-foot ketch, Meotai, in Taiwan; she was launched in 1974 and bought by the Honolulu businessman Bob Burke, who used her as Hokuleʻa’s escort on the voyage of 1976."
     },
     {
      "source": "upi-1976-lompoc",
      "says": "The steel motorsailer Meotai will stay about 10 miles behind the Hokuleʻa and will not assist or communicate with the canoe except in an emergency."
     }
    ],
    "note": "The escort, a steel ketch with an engine, drawn as a motor vessel with her two masts bare. The accounts put her far behind the canoe; where she waited while the canoe left the bay is not given, and here she comes up from off the bay’s mouth onto the canoe’s wake."
   },
